    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d96">

                <pb facs="0296" n="226" sameAs="frus:frus1883/pg226"/>

                <head>No. 96.<lb/><hi rendition="#italic">Mr. Frelinghuysen to Mr.
                    Scruggs.</hi></head>
                <opener><dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >Department of State</hi>,<lb/><hi rendition="#italic"
                                >Washington</hi></placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1882-11-10"><hi rendition="#italic">November</hi> 10,
                        1882</date>.</dateline>
                    <seg rendition="#float-left">No. 31.]</seg></opener>
                <p><hi rendition="#small-caps">Sir</hi>: Your No. 33 of the 2d ultimo, reporting
                    late correspondence with the secretary for foreign affairs, touching the deposit
                    of ships’ papers with the consul of the nation has been received.</p>
                <p>The Department is pleased to see that a question so long under discussion, and in
                    which the justice as well as the convenience of our contention <pb facs="0297"
                        n="227" xml:id="pg227"/> is so evident, is apparently near its close, and
                    will be still more gratified to learn that your last note to his excellency has
                    resulted in completely removing the discordance existing at Panama and Colon,
                    between the federal and the local requirements.</p>
                <p>I am, &amp;c.,</p>
                <closer><signed>FRED’K T. FRELINGHUYSEN.</signed></closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
